  [1mDesign — Section 2: testing and tooling[0m

  The engine is pure, dependency-free logic with a tiny surface, which makes it the single most testable thing in this
  repo — and the dual export exists precisely so it can be loaded in Node. The cases worth covering: all fields valid;
  each field missing individually; both missing; whitespace-only input; a field in the schema but absent from [38;5;153mvalues[39m;
  extra keys in [38;5;153mvalues[39m ignored; a custom message overriding the default; an unknown rule name throwing.

  The repo has [1mno configured tooling at all[0m — [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mscripts[39m, no [38;5;153mdependencies[39m, and no [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m, and
  there's no linter, formatter, test runner, or build step anywhere. That's cheapest to fix now, before the code exists,
  so it's worth a decision rather than an assumption.

  My recommendation for the test runner is Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m with [38;5;153m"test": "node --test"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m: it
  keeps the repo at zero dependencies, needs no config file, and matches the CommonJS already in [38;5;153msrc/[39m. Jest or Vitest
  would each pull in a dependency tree larger than the entire project.

  On the rest: end-to-end tests aren't worth it here — one static page whose only action is a stubbed [38;5;153mlogin()[39m that never
  makes a network call. Fuzz and mutation testing are disproportionate for one [38;5;153mrequired[39m rule. Linting and formatting
  are a genuine judgment call: they'd catch real drift as forms multiply, but they're the first [38;5;153mdevDependencies[39m this
  project would ever take on.

  Implementation will follow TDD — tests written first, watched fail, then the engine.

